The Providence is the strider built at [[Geography/Calaria/Ragged Roost|Ragged Roost]], a platform on eight legs with buildings set on top, three or four stories up. Its name was given by the leader of the [[Phenomena/Pilgrimage|pilgrimage]], who built it with some of his Istrian companions to carry pilgrims to the Void Side Wall.

It stands about three quarters of a mile wide on legs some three hundred feet tall, eight to a side and offset so they clear each other as it walks, which gives it something of a spider or centipede in its build. The lower body holds the machinery that drives the legs. Above it is the city: buildings, shops and a banquet hall toward the middle, and open transplanted grassland around the outer edge, where each keystone-holder is granted a plot of land a quarter mile on a side. Above the buildings, unlike a common strider, rises a fourth layer of interlocking rings set with turning, scoop-shaped apparatus, which the builders added for the long crossing. The rings turn for a few moments each day, their motion as tangled as a double pendulum.

The keystone-holders’ plots lie in a ring around the way down to the lower decks, like the hours of a twenty-place clock, and the twentieth place at the top is kept for [[Org/The Harlequins/People/The Master of Ceremonies|the Master of Ceremonies]]. At the middle of the city stand the shared buildings: a restaurant that charges nothing, a stack of shops, and a five-floor gym of a luxury sort, staffed mostly by [[Bestiary/Fiends|orts]] with Harlequins about as attendants, trainers and sparring partners, and an arena on its upper floor.

Below decks the Providence keeps a parking garage, reached by an elevator with buttons for the garage and the surface only; the engine room is closed to guests. The upper floors hold the banquet hall, which must be reserved, a store, and a small repository where scrolls can be made to order at a higher price than at home. Below deck are the machinery and the basic workman's tools. A Harlequin servant is assigned to each berth, and guests may request an audience with [[Org/The Harlequins/People/The Master of Ceremonies|the Master]], who keeps to himself.

The first guests aboard, at berth 6, are the [[Org/Hildebrandt Corporation/index|Hildebrandt Corporation]], who brought two heavy armored cars tied together on a single [[Phenomena/Keystone|keystone]]; the Party holds berth 14. The Providence set out from Ragged Roost six days after the Party boarded, its rings spinning up to power the first step of the crossing, and held a banquet in the hall the evening it left. Once under way it slips voidward in a jump about every six seconds, each marked by a soft tick and a brief turn of vertigo in those aboard, while its legs also carry it forward over the ground.

A group that leaves the strider can catch up with it again if it stays within about two days of it; beyond that, without the protection of the great ring apparatus on the Providence's back, the crossing wears a traveler down. At a jump every six seconds the wall at the voidward end of the crossing lies some four and a half months, or fifteen tithes, away.

Below the parking garage the Providence carries a lower city of its own. A lever-driven lift drops from the garage through an open column to decks numbered in green paint, the second and third and the twelfth, and on toward a fifteenth and past it into the dark. The halls there are narrow, more like generous vents than corridors, and lit by white panels that blink. The machinery that drives the legs runs close, and each footfall booms up through the metal. Workers live down here, bound to the strider by debts held by [[Org/The Harlequins/index|Harlequins]] that grow with interest, and many carry the marks of the slip. Past the lit halls, beyond banks of acid vats and steam collectors, lies an unlit quarter where [[Bestiary/Mongrel Folk|mongrel folk]] gone far from human shape keep to themselves, and past that a webbed fane.

The strider's working parts are bare in the lower decks: the linkages and gears that drive the legs, and a chamber of eight criss-crossing pistons that still force gas into the ceiling. Webbing from the spiders has gummed some of the linkages. The halls bend and lean the deeper they run, which no six-month-old construction could account for: the Harlequins built the Providence on the hull of an older strider, one that had been adrift far along the Horizon and was taken over long before the pilgrimage was ever planned.

The disused stretch the spiders hold is a fane, the house of a [[Phenomena/Hearts|Heart]]. It is a cylindrical shaft three stories tall, crossed by walkways set at angles to one another, its ceiling hung with the spiders' curtains of silk and their egg sacs.

Past the shaft, up a stair and along branching halls, stands a shrine built of stone where the rest of the strider is metal. It holds six statues: [[Divinity/Hierophant|Aurelia I]] in the vestments of the [[Org/Iridescent Church/index|Iridescent Church]], and her first companions, each in the dress of the god they followed, among them [[People/Culoum Honor-Sworn|Culoum Honor-Sworn]] in the old armor of [[Org/The Scale/index|the Scale]] and the robes of [[Org/Belvedere/index|Belvedere]]. The stone is older than Aurelia II, cut in the first century after the firmament broke, and the room was once a shared place of worship for the six Outer Gods.

The Heart of the fane lies deeper, where the stone gives way to machine. It is the [[Org/The Harlequins/Providence/Machine God|Machine God]], an old Heart in the shape of the broken upper half of a mechanical man, a glowing orb in its chest, fed by pipes that carry a red liquid through four glass vats set about the room with a rune floating in each. It was the eyes and ears of the strider's first inhabitants, and it says the strider was adrift far along the [[Phenomena/Infinite Horizon|Horizon]], a trap that was never sprung, until [[Divinity/Ashen Pact/Herald|Herald]] wormed its way into those people and took and changed them. Followers of Herald still keep to the lower decks, the most active of them on the far side of the strider.
